WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.590
North Korea is perhaps the most hermetic,
closed off and isolated country on the planet.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:23.860
A country that now, in 2018, seems to be living
in 1960.

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:30.140
However, now this entire story may be about
to change.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:34.650
Here at VISUALPOLITIK, we’ve already talked
about how capitalism is reaching North Korea

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:39.410
by following popular markets, how an entire
entrepreneurial revolution is taking place

00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:44.180
and how today more than 50% of North Koreans
make a living from the private economy.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.260
However, a couple of questions remained on
the table...

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:50.989
What does one of the most totalitarian governments
on the planet think about this particular

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:52.030
capitalist revolution?

00:00:52.030 --> 00:00:53.550
What are its plans?

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.920
And another very, very important question.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:59.049
Who the hell would be willing to invest in
North Korea?

00:00:59.049 --> 00:01:02.519
Listen up.

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:12.610
(THESE AREN’T THE BEST OF TIMES)

00:01:12.610 --> 00:01:16.700
On June 12th, 2018, a historic meeting was
held in Singapore...

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:21.050
For the first time, the US President, in this
case Donald Trump, met with a North Korean

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.100
leader.

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:27.150
Why?

00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:32.610
Supposedly to denuclearize North Korea.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:36.049
Perhaps the actual goal?

00:01:36.049 --> 00:01:40.370
Well, in addition to taking a couple of pictures
– hey! more pictures never hurt - starting

00:01:40.370 --> 00:01:45.340
a dialogue, cooling down the enormous tension
that had been growing between them, and preventing

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:48.670
North Korea from continuing with a nuclear
program that could have given it the ability

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:50.479
to attack even the United States.

00:01:50.479 --> 00:01:54.680
Of course... just a moment because theory
is one thing, and truth, something very different.

00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.439
Check out the latest news:

00:01:56.439 --> 00:02:01.750
( “U.S. spy agencies: North Korea is working
on new missiles.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:02.850
Washington Post)

00:02:02.850 --> 00:02:08.060
(“Secretary of State Mike Pompeo acknowledged
during Senate testimony that North Korean

00:02:08.060 --> 00:02:13.870
factories “continue to produce fissile material”
used in making nuclear weapons”.

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:16.340
Washington Post.)

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:22.730
And nowadays, as we told you here on VISUALPOLITIK,
it seems a bit difficult for the Pyongyang

00:02:22.730 --> 00:02:26.650
regime to agree – at least in the short
term – to get rid of all its nuclear weapons,

00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:29.310
which are, after all, their best life insurance.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:32.540
But... of course, that doesn’t mean that
he won’t accept making concessions to normalize

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:36.020
his relations with the rest of the world and
end international sanctions.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:41.340
Because, you see... since Kim Jong Un came
to power in 2011, he’s had two priorities:

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:46.170
developing the nuclear bomb... and something
far less sinister... reforming the economy.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:48.209
Yes, you heard that right.

00:02:48.209 --> 00:02:51.629
Although it seems hard to believe, one of
the objectives that Kim Jong-Un, the North

00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:56.040
Korean dictator, has set himself is to change
his country’s bad economic situation by

00:02:56.040 --> 00:03:01.659
copying some of the changes that have taken
place in countries like China and Vietnam.

00:03:01.659 --> 00:03:05.489
And, check this out, once they develop their
nuclear capabilities…

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:10.689
Pyongyang’s number one priority is precisely
this-- to advance in economic development.

00:03:10.689 --> 00:03:15.269
Of course... to achieve this they need to
end international sanctions... which are causing

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:16.269
enormous damage.

00:03:16.269 --> 00:03:19.870
Yes, we’ll see how, though it may seem hard
to believe, North Korea’s situation could

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:20.870
get worse.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:21.870
For example, check out what happened to the
trade relations between North Korea and Latin

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:24.810
America... between the sanctions and the sinking
of Venezuela - one of the few countries that

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:31.680
had allied itself with North Korea - relations
have collapsed:

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:39.040
But... of course... when we talk about North
Korea and the economy, we need to talk about

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:43.450
China: 80% of all North Korean trade and foreign
exchange occurs here.

00:03:43.450 --> 00:03:48.360
And what’s happened is that China has been
pushing Pyongyang for years to get some reforms

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.930
going once and for all.

00:03:49.930 --> 00:03:54.049
And since those changes have been slower than
a turtle with rheumatism, well… last year

00:03:54.049 --> 00:03:57.140
Beijing decided to start applying international
sanctions...

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:00.680
This is what was happening in August 2017:

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:04.470
(China to Implement UN Sanctions on North
Korea”.

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:05.470
Fox News)

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:09.459
( China bans North Korean iron, lead, coal
imports as part of U.N. sanctions.

00:04:09.459 --> 00:04:10.459
Washington Post)

00:04:10.459 --> 00:04:14.359
As you can imagine, considering the enormous
dependence that North Korea has on China this

00:04:14.359 --> 00:04:17.430
has been a disaster.

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:23.620
Just in the first 6 months of 2018 Chinese
purchases of North Korean products have plummeted

00:04:23.620 --> 00:04:28.560
by more than 90%... and Chinese exports to
North Korea by 40%.

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.850
All this, of course, in the official market...
as we’ve seen, there’s a parallel market,

00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:39.630
the black market, but that's a different story.

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.940
The fact is that all this has led North Korean
industrial and mining production to go through

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:47.940
its worst time in years.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:51.970
And if we add the disastrous state of the
country’s infrastructure to all this...

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:56.310
with their continuous blackouts, less than
1,000 km of paved roads throughout the country

00:04:56.310 --> 00:05:01.100
and decrepit trains that, in many cases, don’t
go over 20 km or 12 miles per hour... it’s

00:05:01.100 --> 00:05:04.470
clear that this country urgently needs a 180-degree
turn...

00:05:04.470 --> 00:05:05.920
And you know what?

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:11.150
They know this in Pyongyang.

00:05:11.150 --> 00:05:20.460
(CAN NORTH KOREA BECOME THE NEW VIETNAM?)

00:05:20.460 --> 00:05:24.610
In 1986 Vietnam launched the well-known Doi
Moi policy, a set of economic reforms similar

00:05:24.610 --> 00:05:29.070
to those of Deng Xiaoping in China, which
had the mission of setting communism aside

00:05:29.070 --> 00:05:33.580
to jump-start a market economy.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.160
To achieve this, agricultural cooperatives
were dissolved, price controls were eliminated,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.890
land ownership was allowed, and policies were
put in place to attract foreign investment.

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:43.990
So what was the result?

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:49.740
Over the following 30 years, Vietnam's economy
grew at an annual average of almost 7%.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:54.260
Today Vietnam is transforming at a breakneck
speed and its exports are already larger than

00:05:54.260 --> 00:06:00.900
those of countries like Brazil or Australia.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:09.720
Well... at the last inter-Korean summit in
April between Kim Jong Un and the president

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:14.050
of South Korea, Moon Jae-in, the North Korean
leader expressed an interest in following

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.090
the model of....

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:16.090
Vietnam.

00:06:16.090 --> 00:06:19.220
And following this line, the North Korean
government has approved some significant measures

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.510
in recent years:

00:06:20.510 --> 00:06:25.380
for example, it has promoted the creation
of private markets, has given more autonomy

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:29.780
to public companies and has granted land parcels
for the peasants’ private exploitation.

00:06:29.780 --> 00:06:34.220
However... despite all this, there are two
questions we need to answer: Who on earth

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.180
would be willing to invest in North Korea?

00:06:36.180 --> 00:06:39.040
And what else is the Pyongyang government
willing to do?

00:06:39.040 --> 00:06:42.350
Listen up.

00:06:42.350 --> 00:06:51.600
(WHO WOULD INVEST IN NORTH KOREA?)

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.690
If you were asked which countries you’d
like to do business in, North Korea would

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:57.650
probably be one of your last options...

00:06:57.650 --> 00:07:01.570
Investing in such a totalitarian country,
where the leader makes and unmakes at will,

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:05.560
where the government acts with brutality and
dedicates itself to threatening the rest of

00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:06.560
the planet?

00:07:06.560 --> 00:07:09.710
Investing in a country where the rule of law
and legal security are pure fantasy?

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:10.710
Come on!

00:07:10.710 --> 00:07:15.230
Well... folks, the truth is that not everyone
sees it this way.

00:07:15.230 --> 00:07:21.200
See, some of South Korea's largest companies
such as Hyundai, Lotte Corporation and Samsung

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:26.120
have already set up workgroups to investigate
and analyze potential business opportunities

00:07:26.120 --> 00:07:27.120
in North Korea.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.740
And according to polls, three-quarters of
South Korean companies would be willing to

00:07:30.740 --> 00:07:33.750
invest in the north if sanctions were lifted.

00:07:33.750 --> 00:07:38.400
Hyundai, for example, is already preparing
an early reopening of the industrial Kaesong

00:07:38.400 --> 00:07:42.611
zone, an industrial zone next to the border
of South Korea that was created in 2004 to

00:07:42.611 --> 00:07:47.190
house foreign companies – mainly South Korean
– and which employed more than 80,000 North

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:48.190
Koreans...

00:07:48.190 --> 00:07:52.360
But in 2013, given the growing tensions between
the two Koreas, it was closed by the Pyongyang

00:07:52.360 --> 00:07:53.730
government.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:59.650
Even recently, Jim Rogers, one of the most
important and well-known investors of all

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:04.010
time, has lamented the fact that the United
States doesn’t allow him to invest in North

00:08:04.010 --> 00:08:06.770
Korea and has made statements such as these:

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:10.670
(“Korea is now going to be the most exciting
country in the world.

00:08:10.670 --> 00:08:12.970
Kim Jong-Un does not want war.

00:08:12.970 --> 00:08:14.360
Kim is not North Korean.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:16.490
He was educated in Switzerland.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:18.270
He knows there’s a different world out there.

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:21.240
“They need everything … electricity … soap
… tablecloths,...

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:24.820
[...] If you’ve got any kind of expertise
you’re going to make a fortune.”

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:25.820
Jim Rogers)

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:29.760
But wait just a moment... the question we
have to ask ourselves is, what the hell do

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:32.700
they see in a country like North Korea?

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:34.930
Well... fundamentally, opportunities.

00:08:34.930 --> 00:08:37.890
Allow me to explain.

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:43.120
North Korea has a relatively young, very entrepreneurial
population with very low labor costs.

00:08:43.120 --> 00:08:47.940
In addition, the country seems to be quite
rich in natural resources: gold, zinc, copper,

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:54.009
rare earth elements… the estimated value
of all mineral resources is more than 6 billion

00:08:54.009 --> 00:08:55.490
dollars.

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:59.800
Then, as Jim Rogers says, the country is being
built.

00:08:59.800 --> 00:09:01.380
It needs everything.

00:09:01.380 --> 00:09:05.240
And if that weren’t enough, it’s also
located between two economic powers, China

00:09:05.240 --> 00:09:06.240
and South Korea.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:09.870
See, at the last inter-Korean summit, the
South Korean President gave Kim Jong-Un a

00:09:09.870 --> 00:09:14.970
pen drive that included lots of the investments
that South Korea would be willing to make.

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:18.519
Do you know what investments were included
on this pen drive?

00:09:18.519 --> 00:09:22.120
Well, for example, Seoul would be very interested
in building trans-Korean railroads that, in

00:09:22.120 --> 00:09:26.240
addition to boosting the North Korean economy,
would allow South Korea to export goods much

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.339
more quickly to China.

00:09:27.339 --> 00:09:34.769
And the same with roads, oil pipelines, etc.,
etc.... these would be billion-dollar plans.

00:09:34.769 --> 00:09:39.430
Of course... not everything is so simple and
enthusiasm is far from widespread.

00:09:39.430 --> 00:09:44.529
North Korea is a brutal dictatorship, corruption
is huge and the government can do, literally

00:09:44.529 --> 00:09:45.529
whatever it wants.

00:09:45.529 --> 00:09:46.529
For example, expropriate:

00:09:46.529 --> 00:09:47.529
(TEXT: Well, be careful with your answers,
you know?

00:09:47.529 --> 00:09:48.920
Because you’re telling me that the bank
isn’t for sale but I can expropriate it

00:09:48.920 --> 00:09:53.180
if I want.)

00:09:53.180 --> 00:10:01.209
(“This is where the money is to be made…
if you can figure out how not to get expropriated.”

00:10:01.209 --> 00:10:05.089
Justin Hastings, an associate professor at
the University of Sydney)

00:10:05.089 --> 00:10:08.949
And then, of course, there’s the issue of
international sanctions... as a consequence

00:10:08.949 --> 00:10:10.120
of the nuclear program.

00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:13.720
And, until they are lifted… any change will
have a very short run.

00:10:13.720 --> 00:10:17.249
Now, it’s precisely at this point that China
can play a very important role.

00:10:17.249 --> 00:10:21.529
You see, Xi Jinping has managed to do what
China’s been planning for years: getting

00:10:21.529 --> 00:10:22.980
Pyongyang going.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.699
Which could obviously mean big business for
Chinese companies.

00:10:25.699 --> 00:10:29.000
That’s why we now see headlines like this.

00:10:29.000 --> 00:10:34.160
(China suggests sanctions relief for North
Korea after Trump-Kim summit.

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:35.160
CNBC)

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:38.480
And not only that, China has allowed the black
market between both countries to flourish,

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:43.029
has accelerated the development plans in the
border area and is already studying the imminent

00:10:43.029 --> 00:10:46.130
opening of special economic zones on the North
Korean border...

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.540
And then there’s tourism... which is precisely
one of Kim Jong Un's government’s greatest

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:50.920
priorities.

00:10:50.920 --> 00:10:53.680
Listen up.

00:10:53.680 --> 00:11:00.640
(NORTH KOREA, A HOLIDAY SPOT?)

00:11:00.640 --> 00:11:04.889
The arrival of Chinese tourists has continued
growing in recent years and since May 2019

00:11:04.889 --> 00:11:07.120
the number of daily arrivals has more than
doubled.

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.300
(“We are going to run at least seven or
eight tour routes to North Korea this year,

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:18.180
including a one-day tour to Nanyo and a trip
of four to five days to Pyongyang, Kaesong

00:11:18.180 --> 00:11:19.180
and Panmunjom”.

00:11:19.180 --> 00:11:23.139
- Che Jing, a deputy manager of a chinese
travel company.)

00:11:23.139 --> 00:11:27.540
Well... tourism is precisely one of the Pyongyang
government’s top priorities... for several

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:28.540
reasons:

00:11:28.540 --> 00:11:33.579
first it generates employment – remember
this is a very labor-intensive sector – attracts

00:11:33.579 --> 00:11:37.740
investment and helps obtain foreign currency
quickly.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:43.660
On the other hand tourism could change the
regime’s image… and, the truth is that

00:11:43.660 --> 00:11:48.050
swapping nuclear parades and nuclear threats
with exotic beaches and seaside dinners…

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:49.430
would be good for business.

00:11:49.430 --> 00:11:53.890
Due to this, in 2014 the North Korean government
created the Special Tourist Zone of Wonsan,

00:11:53.890 --> 00:11:58.060
a special economic zone where foreign investment
is allowed and where in principle, the regime

00:11:58.060 --> 00:12:00.630
would allow more freedom for developing tourism.

00:12:00.630 --> 00:12:05.170
Yes, this is the same area where until recently
the army did things like this.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:11.250
You know, North Korean things.

00:12:11.250 --> 00:12:23.730
The fact is that with the nuclear program
and the sanctions, results have been very

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:26.689
scarce... however Kim Jong Un wants to step
on the gas.

00:12:26.689 --> 00:12:31.120
Without going any further, in 2017 the North
Korean government sent a delegation to Spain,

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:35.459
which is one of the major tourist powers of
the world, to check out places like Benidorm

00:12:35.459 --> 00:12:40.069
or Marina d'Or [Marina Door], to learn from
them and get some ideas.

00:12:40.069 --> 00:12:42.990
You heard that right.

00:12:42.990 --> 00:12:47.300
Apparently, Pyongyang’s goal is to attract
more than 1 million tourists per year in the

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:50.550
short term and 5 to 10 million in the reasonable
future.

00:12:50.550 --> 00:12:54.620
The question is, will Kim Jong-Un really be
willing to carry out all the reforms that

00:12:54.620 --> 00:12:57.800
the country needs so that all these plans
can come to life?

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:00.639
Folks, only the future can answer that question.

00:13:00.639 --> 00:13:04.149
For now, what seems clear is that we have
the best opportunity to leave the North Korean

00:13:04.149 --> 00:13:09.230
nightmare behind, which isn’t only a threat
to the rest of the world but above all a punishment

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:12.439
and a great prison of fear and repression
for North Koreans themselves.

00:13:12.439 --> 00:13:16.899
But now... it’s your turn, are you in favor
of lifting sanctions against North Korea?

00:13:16.899 --> 00:13:19.570
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:23.791
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

